[   22.360] What went wrong[0m
[   22.363] Immucore checks the OS image against the sha256 recorded for it
[   22.366] before it mounts it, and the check did not pass.
[   22.366] 
[   22.366]   Problem:
[   22.366]     the check ran out of time
[   22.366]   Image:
[   22.366]     /sysroot/run/initramfs/cos-state/cOS/active.img
[   22.366]   Expected sha256 (stored in active.img.sha256):
[   22.366]     815a1742f6fb57754bd13bad0a1576fada27589f941b0c3397a90cafced92742
[   22.366]   Actual sha256 (computed from the image just now):
[   22.366]     (not available)
[   22.366] 
[   22.367]   Hashing stopped after 5.005s (529530880 bytes read) because image-checksum.verify-timeout
[   22.368]   is 5s, so the image was not verified. require is on, so it counts as a failure.
[   22.368] 
[   22.369]   Action: fail (from image-checksum.conf on the state partition)
[   22.369]   A missing or stale checksum is a problem too: require is on
[   22.369]   (from image-checksum.conf on the state partition)
[   22.369] 
[   22.369] How to fix
[   22.369]   - Reboot and pick the passive or recovery entry in the boot menu.
[   22.370]     Then repair the image with kairos-agent upgrade or reset.
[   22.371]   - To boot this image anyway, edit the boot entry (press e) and add
[   22.371]     image-checksum.verify=warn to the kernel line.
[   22.371]   - A disk that corrupts images can corrupt more: check its health.
[   22.371] 
[   22.371] Where to look
[   22.372]   journalctl -b -u immucore
[   22.372]   /run/immucore/
